\section{Leakage controls}{

The held-out domain's evaluation rows never reach fitting, calibration or
any readout fit; they are only encoded and scored. The \code{target} column is
removed from the calibration design, so calibration never sees held-out
labels. Source designs are passed unchanged (source labels are permitted
training data). With \code{evaluation_groups = "shared"} the source domains keep
their observations of the held-out evaluation events: this measures
transfer to a new domain for events the reference has seen. With
\code{evaluation_groups = "novel"} (requires \code{event_id}) source rows of the
held-out evaluation events are removed before fitting and before the target
readout is trained, which measures transfer to a new domain and new events.
In both modes, source rows of evaluation events are used only as prediction
inputs for \code{recon_transfer_r2} and as prediction targets for
\code{recon_source_r2}, and are always excluded from fitting the source readouts
\code{D_s}.
}

\section{Scores}{

All readouts are ridge-regularised linear least-squares fits with an
intercept (ridge \code{readout_ridge} times the mean diagonal of the centred
latent Gram matrix).
\describe{
\item{\code{target_score}}{the target readout is fitted on encoded source rows
with a non-missing \code{target} and applied to the encoded held-out
evaluation rows. A numeric target is scored by out-of-sample R-squared
relative to the source training mean (\code{target_metric = "r2"},
\code{target_baseline = 0}). Any other target is a class label scored by
accuracy (\code{target_metric = "accuracy"}) with a one-hot linear readout
and arg-max prediction; \code{target_baseline} is the accuracy of always
predicting the most frequent source class. A custom \code{target_readout}
replaces the linear readout.}
\item{\code{recon_self_r2}}{an explicit linear readout \code{D_h} from latent
coordinates back to the held-out domain's native columns is fitted on
the calibration rows; held-out evaluation rows are reconstructed from
their own encodings. R-squared is pooled over columns, relative to the
calibration column means.}
\item{\code{recon_transfer_r2}}{(requires \code{event_id}) each evaluation row is
predicted through \code{D_h} from the source domains' consensus encoding of
its event: the mean over source domains of each domain's mean encoding
of that event. Rows whose event no source domain observed are not
scored; \code{n_transfer} counts the rows scored.}
\item{\code{recon_source_r2}}{(requires \code{event_id}) for each source domain \code{s}
an explicit readout \code{D_s} back to its native columns is fitted on its
encoded rows outside the evaluation events; its rows of evaluation
events are predicted from the held-out domain's mean evaluation encoding
of the same event. R-squared is pooled over source domains and columns,
relative to each \code{D_s} training mean.}
}
Every method is therefore scored in the same outcome spaces (the target and
each domain's native columns), whatever its latent dimension.
}

\section{Status}{

Each fold and method gives one row. \code{status} is \code{"ok"}, \code{"unsupported"}
(the method cannot calibrate a new domain against a frozen fit or cannot
encode new rows; \code{message} gives the reason) or \code{"failed"} (an error was
